15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 30, 1862","place":"The Glendale crossroads and adjoining farms, woods, and roads between White Oak Swamp and Malvern Hill, east and southeast of Richmond, Virginia","result":"Indecisive. Longstreet’s and A. P. Hill’s attacks broke George A. McCall’s Pennsylvania Reserves and captured artillery, but United States reinforcements held the Willis Church Road. Lee did not cut the Army of the Potomac’s line to the James River, and the Federal army withdrew to Malvern Hill during the night","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The June 30 fighting at Glendale and the withdrawal to Malvern Hill","note":"Episode 164 covered the approach to Glendale and the separate action at White Oak Swamp. This episode completes the battle and follows the Army of the Potomac’s night movement south."},{"label":"Battle names","value":"Glendale, Frayser’s Farm, Nelson’s Farm, and Charles City Crossroads","note":"The National Park Service records several additional contemporary names, including New Market Crossroads, Riddell’s Shop, and White Oak Swamp. United States and Confederate accounts did not use one uniform name."},{"label":"Confederate objective","value":"Sever the Willis Church Road and divide the retreating Federal army","note":"The crossroads controlled the principal route south to Malvern Hill and the James River. Lee attempted to converge separate columns on that bottleneck."},{"label":"Huger’s action","value":"Stopped opposite Slocum on the Charles City Road","note":"After clearing the road obstructions, Benjamin Huger brought up only two guns for an unequal artillery exchange and did not order an infantry assault. Slocum later sent George W. Taylor’s brigade toward Glendale."},{"label":"Holmes at Malvern Hill","value":"Artillery fire failed to disrupt the Federal wagon route","note":"United States batteries and gunboats on the James River drove off Theophilus H. Holmes’s guns and disordered his inexperienced infantry. Holmes did not attack the strong Malvern Hill position."},{"label":"Magruder’s movements","value":"Repeated orders kept 13,500 men out of the main Glendale fighting","note":"The episode describes seven sets of orders and about 20 miles of marching and countermarching. Magruder reached the Glendale front after the battle, at about 3 a.m. on July 1."},{"label":"Main Confederate striking force","value":"Longstreet’s division and A. P. Hill’s Light Division","note":"These formations approached from the west by the Long Bridge Road and carried out the principal infantry attacks against McCall, Kearny, Hooker, and arriving elements of Sedgwick and Slocum."},{"label":"Opening of the main assault","value":"Micah Jenkins attacked at about 4 p.m.","note":"Longstreet ordered Jenkins to silence James H. Cooper’s battery. Jenkins advanced his whole brigade, beginning a general engagement before Lee or Longstreet had ordered the coordinated assault they intended."},{"label":"Jenkins’s attack","value":"Three charges against Cooper’s battery and Seymour’s brigade","note":"Jenkins’s South Carolinians reached the gun line and briefly took the battery before a Federal counterattack forced them away. The episode gives more than 500 casualties from about 1,200 men."},{"label":"Palmetto Sharpshooters","value":"254 casualties from 375 engaged in the episode’s account","note":"The episode gives 44 killed and 210 wounded, or about 68 percent of the regiment engaged. Campaign and battle casualty reports do not always separate losses in the same way."},{"label":"Kemper’s advance","value":"A rapid, unsupported charge reached the Whitlock clearing","note":"Kemper’s brigade crossed about 1,200 yards of woods and clearings, broke part of Seymour’s left, and reached ground near the Whitlock house. Its speed disrupted its formation and separated it from supporting brigades."},{"label":"Kemper’s losses","value":"More than 400 of roughly 1,400 men in the episode’s account","note":"A Federal counterattack drove the unsupported Virginians back. The total includes killed, wounded, and captured soldiers."},{"label":"Near-breakthrough","value":"Branch and Strange advanced within a few hundred yards of Willis Church Road","note":"Lawrence O. Branch’s brigade and George Pickett’s brigade, led by Col. John B. Strange while Pickett recovered from a wound, retook the Whitlock clearing and pursued the Federal line eastward."},{"label":"Sedgwick’s reinforcement","value":"Dana’s and Sully’s brigades reached the threatened sector in time","note":"The two brigades returned from the White Oak Swamp sector and entered the fight as they arrived. Their fire, followed by pressure from Hooker’s direction, stopped Branch and Strange before they reached the road."},{"label":"Fight for Randol’s battery","value":"Wilcox attacked Battery E and G, 1st U.S. Artillery","note":"Capt. Alanson M. Randol’s guns were supported by George G. Meade’s brigade. Prolonged close combat cost the two attacking Alabama regiments more than 250 casualties in the episode’s account, and Meade was wounded."},{"label":"Pryor and Featherston","value":"Their attacks stalled in wooded terrain against Kearny’s division","note":"Richard Pryor fed regiments forward against artillery and John C. Robinson’s infantry. Winfield S. Featherston extended the line but could not advance, and Maxcy Gregg committed only part of his brigade in support."},{"label":"McCall captured","value":"Taken by soldiers of the 47th Virginia","note":"While trying to organize another line, the wounded division commander rode into Confederate troops and was captured. His Pennsylvania Reserves had already fought at Beaver Dam Creek and Gaines’s Mill."},{"label":"Kearny’s escape","value":"Rode out of a group of Field’s Confederates after being mistaken for a Confederate officer","note":"Kearny was unarmed and used a verbal bluff to leave the group. The episode distinguishes this escape from his death two months later at Chantilly under different circumstances."},{"label":"Captured artillery","value":"More than a dozen guns in the main breakthrough; 18 in the episode’s total","note":"The National Park Service states that Confederates captured more than a dozen cannon. The episode counts 14 retained near the Cooper and Randol battery line and 18 across the battle."},{"label":"Final Confederate attacks","value":"Field, Pender, Archer, and Anderson did not reach Willis Church Road","note":"Field withdrew after Taylor threatened his flank. Pender met Sedgwick’s last defensive line, Archer stalled against Hooker, and Joseph R. Anderson’s after-dark advance lost cohesion in the woods."},{"label":"Battle casualties","value":"Approximately 3,500 on each side","note":"Richmond National Battlefield Park emphasizes that June 30 totals are uncertain. The episode uses about 3,800 United States and 3,700 Confederate casualties; another widely used compilation gives 3,797 and 3,673, respectively."},{"label":"Senior command casualties","value":"McCall captured; Simmons killed; Meade and several generals wounded","note":"Col. Seneca G. Simmons, commanding a Pennsylvania Reserves brigade, was fatally wounded or killed during the counterattack. NPS also identifies United States generals Meade and Sumner and Confederate generals Joseph R. Anderson, Pender, and Featherston as wounded."},{"label":"Immediate result","value":"The Willis Church Road remained open","note":"The Confederate penetration did not become a sustained breakthrough. Federal formations withdrew south after the fighting rather than being cut off west or north of the crossroads."},{"label":"Night withdrawal","value":"The Army of the Potomac moved to Malvern Hill","note":"The movement was poorly coordinated and some pickets were left behind, but the main body reached the strong position a little more than two miles south of Glendale before the July 1 battle."},{"label":"Forces actually engaged","value":"Fewer than 20,000 Confederates against more than 23,000 Federals in the episode’s estimate","note":"These figures describe troops that entered the main fighting, not all soldiers present in the broader June 30 operational area. Jackson, Huger, Holmes, and Magruder did not add infantry to Longstreet and Hill at Glendale."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“A Glorious Army: Robert E. Lee’s Triumph, 1862–1863” by Jeffry D. Wert","note":"The official episode page and podcast book list identify this title as the recommendation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Huger, Kearny, Heintzelman, Randol, Maxcy Gregg, and Jeffry D. Wert","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “U.J.,” “Carney,” “Heinzelman,” “Randall,” “Maxie,” and “Jeffrey D. Wirt.” “U.J.” refers to Benjamin Huger."}],"maps":[{"title":"Glendale in the Peninsula Campaign","caption":"Glendale lay east and southeast of Richmond on the Army of the Potomac’s route toward Malvern Hill and the James River. The inset shows the north-to-south sequence from White Oak Swamp through Glendale. Coastlines, waterways, routes, locations, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep165-regional-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map placing Richmond and Glendale in eastern Virginia and showing the Federal withdrawal corridor to Malvern Hill and the James River."},{"title":"Main action west of the Glendale crossroads","caption":"McCall’s Pennsylvania Reserves held west of Willis Church Road, with Kearny and Hooker on the flanks. Jenkins, Kemper, Wilcox, Branch, Strange, Pryor, Featherston, Field, Pender, Archer, and Anderson attacked in separate movements from the west. Dana, Sully, and Taylor reinforced the defense. Arrows and formation bars show generalized relationships, not exact routes, timing, frontages, or troop geometry. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep165-glendale-movements.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Federal defensive sectors, Confederate brigade attacks, and Federal reinforcements west of the Glendale crossroads on June 30, 1862."},{"title":"Alfred Waud’s Glendale field sketch","caption":"Alfred R. Waud made this pencil-and-Chinese-white drawing at Glendale on June 30, 1862. The Library of Congress title is supplied from an inscription on the reverse. It is a participant-era visual record, not a surveyed battlefield plan.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep165-waud-glendale.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of soldiers and the battlefield landscape at Glendale, Virginia, on June 30, 1862."}],"moments":[{"time":"7:23","audioTime":443,"title":"The battle date and crossroads are reviewed","detail":"The episode returns to Glendale on Monday, June 30, and the Federal line of retreat toward the James River."},{"time":"8:12","audioTime":492,"title":"Lee’s operational objective is restated","detail":"Lee seeks to break the Federal line at the bottleneck and cut off part of McClellan’s army."},{"time":"8:53","audioTime":533,"title":"The three converging movements are identified","detail":"Jackson is north at White Oak Swamp, Huger approaches by Charles City Road, and Longstreet and A. P. Hill approach from the west."},{"time":"10:55","audioTime":655,"title":"Federal brigades leave Jackson’s front","detail":"The episode states that four brigades, nearly 10,000 men, move from the White Oak sector and later reinforce Glendale."},{"time":"11:15","audioTime":675,"title":"Huger reaches Slocum’s position","detail":"Between about 2:30 and 3 p.m., Huger finds Federal infantry and artillery on a ridge across the creek."},{"time":"11:48","audioTime":708,"title":"Huger’s two guns are suppressed","detail":"Federal counterbattery fire overwhelms the small Confederate artillery deployment, and no infantry assault follows."},{"time":"13:06","audioTime":786,"title":"Slocum releases Taylor’s brigade","detail":"Huger’s failure to attack allows one VI Corps brigade to reinforce the main Glendale line."},{"time":"14:02","audioTime":842,"title":"Longstreet and Hill reach the Federal pickets","detail":"Their columns halt on the Long Bridge Road at about 11 a.m., roughly one mile short of Glendale."},{"time":"15:26","audioTime":926,"title":"Artillery fire is mistaken for the attack signal","detail":"Lee and Longstreet hear firing to the north and assume that Huger has begun the planned coordinated action."},{"time":"16:35","audioTime":995,"title":"Lee inspects the Malvern Hill sector","detail":"A report of Federal troops and wagons moving over Malvern Hill prompts Lee to ride toward the River Road."},{"time":"17:12","audioTime":1032,"title":"Holmes opens against Malvern Hill","detail":"About six rifled guns shell the Federal position and wagon route before land batteries and gunboats reply."},{"time":"17:48","audioTime":1068,"title":"Federal fire drives off Holmes’s force","detail":"Gunboat and artillery shells scatter the Confederate guns and cause inexperienced infantry to retreat in disorder."},{"time":"19:18","audioTime":1158,"title":"Magruder is redirected toward Holmes","detail":"Lee diverts Magruder from supporting Longstreet and Hill because he believes added force may be needed at Malvern Hill."},{"time":"21:13","audioTime":1273,"title":"Magruder’s marches are summarized","detail":"Successive changes send the division between the River Road and Glendale; it reaches the front only after the battle."},{"time":"22:36","audioTime":1356,"title":"The main battle begins from a misunderstood order","detail":"Longstreet’s direction to silence a battery leads Micah Jenkins to advance his full South Carolina brigade."},{"time":"23:47","audioTime":1427,"title":"Jenkins attacks Cooper’s battery","detail":"At about 4 p.m., the South Carolinians move against Cooper’s guns and Truman Seymour’s Pennsylvania Reserves."},{"time":"24:32","audioTime":1472,"title":"Three charges reach the gun line","detail":"Jenkins’s brigade enters hand-to-hand fighting, briefly takes the battery, and then loses the guns to a Federal counterattack."},{"time":"25:30","audioTime":1530,"title":"Jenkins’s losses are counted","detail":"The episode gives more than 500 brigade casualties and 254 casualties in the Palmetto Sharpshooters."},{"time":"25:59","audioTime":1559,"title":"Longstreet orders the other brigades forward","detail":"The wider Confederate attack begins at about 5 p.m. after Jenkins has already opened the engagement."},{"time":"26:16","audioTime":1576,"title":"Kemper charges toward the Whitlock house","detail":"The Virginia brigade moves rapidly through woods and clearings, loses formation, and strikes Seymour’s exposed left."},{"time":"28:38","audioTime":1718,"title":"A Federal counterattack drives Kemper back","detail":"Three regiments attack the unsupported Virginians, who withdraw with more than 400 casualties in the episode’s account."},{"time":"30:13","audioTime":1813,"title":"Branch and Strange renew the advance","detail":"Their brigades cross the Whitlock clearing and move within a few hundred yards of Willis Church Road."},{"time":"31:10","audioTime":1870,"title":"Sedgwick’s brigades reach the threatened line","detail":"Dana’s and Sully’s troops enter the action and help stop the Confederate movement toward the road."},{"time":"32:32","audioTime":1952,"title":"Wilcox attacks along the Long Bridge Road","detail":"Two Alabama regiments support Jenkins while two others attack Randol’s battery and Meade’s brigade."},{"time":"33:09","audioTime":1989,"title":"Meade is wounded","detail":"Close combat around Randol’s gun line forces Meade to leave the field; Wilcox’s attacking regiments later withdraw."},{"time":"33:55","audioTime":2035,"title":"Pryor attacks Kearny’s sector","detail":"Wooded terrain breaks up the advance, and Federal canister and Robinson’s infantry stop the Confederate regiments."},{"time":"34:52","audioTime":2092,"title":"Featherston and Gregg extend the Confederate left","detail":"Featherston joins Pryor but cannot advance; Gregg commits one regiment and does not make a general attack."},{"time":"35:48","audioTime":2148,"title":"Field enters near the batteries","detail":"Field’s brigade advances as Wilcox withdraws and presses toward Randol’s and Cooper’s positions."},{"time":"36:20","audioTime":2180,"title":"McCall is captured","detail":"The division commander rides among soldiers of the 47th Virginia while trying to place another Federal line."},{"time":"37:06","audioTime":2226,"title":"Kearny escapes from Field’s troops","detail":"Mistaken for a Confederate officer, Kearny gives an order and rides away before his identity is recognized."},{"time":"38:00","audioTime":2280,"title":"Confederates retain the battery line","detail":"Field overruns Randol’s guns while other regiments retake Cooper’s battery; the episode counts 14 guns held in this sector."},{"time":"38:35","audioTime":2315,"title":"Taylor threatens Field’s flank","detail":"The New Jersey brigade from Slocum’s division attacks from the left and leads Field to withdraw to a safer position."},{"time":"39:05","audioTime":2345,"title":"Pender strikes the last Federal line","detail":"Pender advances beyond the Whitlock house but cannot dislodge Sedgwick’s troops near Willis Church Road."},{"time":"39:55","audioTime":2395,"title":"Archer’s attack stalls against Hooker","detail":"Archer holds ground south of the Whitlock house without breaking the Federal division to his front and right."},{"time":"40:13","audioTime":2413,"title":"Anderson advances after dark","detail":"Smoke, woods, and failing light prevent the final A. P. Hill brigade from maintaining a coherent attack."},{"time":"41:02","audioTime":2462,"title":"Battle casualties are assessed","detail":"The episode uses about 3,700 Confederate and 3,800 United States casualties while noting the difficulty of separating campaign losses."},{"time":"41:27","audioTime":2487,"title":"Captured guns are totaled","detail":"The episode counts 18 Federal guns taken across the June 30 fighting."},{"time":"41:36","audioTime":2496,"title":"The Confederate objective remains unmet","detail":"Lee’s attacks have not severed Willis Church Road or cut off part of the Army of the Potomac."},{"time":"42:34","audioTime":2554,"title":"McClellan reaches Malvern Hill","detail":"At about 8:30 p.m., McClellan arrives at Fitz John Porter’s headquarters as the Glendale fighting ends."},{"time":"43:43","audioTime":2623,"title":"The troops actually engaged are compared","detail":"The episode estimates fewer than 20,000 Confederate attackers against more than 23,000 Federal defenders."},{"time":"43:56","audioTime":2636,"title":"The Federal withdrawal resumes","detail":"The Glendale defenders leave their lines during the night and move south on Willis Church Road."},{"time":"45:10","audioTime":2710,"title":"The column reaches Malvern Hill","detail":"The retreating troops climb the hill before dawn, where the final major battle of the Seven Days will occur on July 1."},{"time":"45:33","audioTime":2733,"title":"The episode’s book recommendation is given","detail":"The hosts recommend Jeffry D. Wert’s “A Glorious Army: Robert E. Lee’s Triumph, 1862–1863.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Was at Haxall’s Landing and aboard USS Galena during the main fighting; reached Fitz John Porter’s Malvern Hill headquarters at about 8:30 p.m."},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"John Sedgwick’s division supplied Napoleon J. T. Dana’s and Alfred Sully’s brigades to the decisive sector near Willis Church Road"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman","command":"III Corps","subordinates":"Philip Kearny’s division, including John C. Robinson’s brigade, held north of McCall; Joseph Hooker’s division held south and southeast of McCall"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George A. McCall","command":"Pennsylvania Reserves division, V Corps","subordinates":"Brigades of Truman Seymour, George G. Meade, and Col. Seneca G. Simmons held the central sector; McCall was wounded and captured"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Henry W. Slocum","command":"1st Division, VI Corps","subordinates":"Held Huger on the Charles City Road and sent George W. Taylor’s New Jersey brigade to strike Field’s flank"},{"commander":"Capts. James H. Cooper and Alanson M. Randol","command":"Battery B, 1st Pennsylvania Light Artillery, and Batteries E and G, 1st U.S. Artillery","subordinates":"Their gun lines were the focus of repeated attacks by Jenkins, Wilcox, and Field"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the converging operation but diverted Magruder toward Holmes and did not bring Jackson or Huger into the main battle"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Longstreet’s division and the western attack","subordinates":"Brigades under Micah Jenkins, James L. Kemper, Cadmus M. Wilcox, Roger A. Pryor, and Winfield S. Featherston"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division","subordinates":"Brigades under Lawrence O. Branch, Charles W. Field, William D. Pender, James J. Archer, Joseph R. Anderson, and Maxcy Gregg"},{"commander":"Col. John B. Strange","command":"George E. Pickett’s brigade","subordinates":"Led Pickett’s brigade while Pickett recovered from a Gaines’s Mill wound; advanced with Branch toward Willis Church Road"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin Huger","command":"Division on the Charles City Road","subordinates":"Conducted an unsuccessful artillery exchange against Slocum and did not enter the main infantry battle"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Column at White Oak Swamp","subordinates":"Did not cross Franklin’s position or prevent Federal brigades from reinforcing Glendale"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John B. Magruder","command":"Reserve","subordinates":"Marched between the Darbytown Road, River Road, and Glendale under repeated changes of orders and arrived after the battle"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Theophilus H. Holmes","command":"Division near Malvern Hill","subordinates":"His artillery and infantry withdrew under Federal land-battery and gunboat fire"}]}],"people":[{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate division commander directing the principal western assault","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep165-james-longstreet.jpg"},{"name":"George G. Meade","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves brigade commander wounded in the fight around Randol’s battery","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep165-george-meade.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who sought to cut the Federal withdrawal route at Glendale","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Light Division commander whose six brigades entered the main Glendale action","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George A. McCall","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves division commander wounded and captured during the Confederate breakthrough","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"III Corps division commander whose troops held north of McCall and stopped Pryor and Featherston","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"III Corps division commander whose troops held south of McCall and pressured the Confederate right","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Sedgwick","role":"II Corps division commander whose returning brigades reinforced the line near Willis Church Road","side":"United States"},{"name":"Micah Jenkins","role":"South Carolina brigade commander whose advance against Cooper’s battery opened the main battle","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James L. Kemper","role":"Virginia brigade commander whose rapid advance reached the Whitlock clearing before being counterattacked","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Cadmus M. Wilcox","role":"Alabama brigade commander who attacked Cooper’s and Randol’s battery sectors","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Lawrence O. Branch","role":"A. P. Hill brigade commander who advanced with Pickett’s brigade toward Willis Church Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Charles W. Field","role":"A. P. Hill brigade commander who overran Federal guns before Taylor threatened his flank","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William D. Pender","role":"A. P. Hill brigade commander stopped by Sedgwick’s defensive line near Willis Church Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Benjamin Huger","role":"Confederate division commander stopped opposite Slocum on the Charles City Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Magruder","role":"Confederate reserve commander redirected between the River Road and Glendale","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Theophilus H. Holmes","role":"Confederate division commander repulsed by Federal artillery and gunboats near Malvern Hill","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Seneca G. Simmons","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves brigade commander killed during the battle","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. Seneca G. Simmons","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 1st Brigade, Pennsylvania Reserves division","outcome":"Fatally wounded or killed while leading the counterattack against Kemper’s brigade. Contemporary and later records differ on whether he died on the field or during the following night; the official army register records his death at the June 30 battle."},{"name":"Capt. Henry J. Biddle","side":"United States","role":"Assistant adjutant general, Pennsylvania Reserves division","outcome":"Mortally wounded at Glendale and captured. He was taken to Chimborazo Hospital in Richmond and died on July 20, 1862."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep165-regional-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map placing the Glendale battlefield east of Richmond and on the route toward the James River.","caption":"Glendale stood between the Army of the Potomac and its next defensive position at Malvern Hill. Locations, coastlines, routes, and distances are generalized.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden maps, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep165-glendale-movements.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the main Glendale attacks and Federal defensive sectors west of Willis Church Road.","caption":"Longstreet’s and A. P. Hill’s brigades attacked in separate movements from the west. Federal reinforcements prevented the penetration from reaching Willis Church Road. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Historic American Landscapes Survey, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep165-waud-glendale.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of soldiers and the landscape at Glendale on June 30, 1862.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew this view at Glendale on the day of the battle. The inscription on the reverse identifies the place and date.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep165-waud-kearny.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing titled Fighting in the woods, Kearny’s division repulsing the enemy, June 30, 1862.","caption":"Waud’s field drawing records Kearny’s division fighting in the wooded Glendale terrain. It was later published in Harper’s Weekly.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep165-james-longstreet.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of Confederate general James Longstreet.","caption":"James Longstreet directed the principal Confederate assault from the west with his division and A. P. Hill’s Light Division.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep165-george-meade.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era carte-de-visite portrait of United States general George G. Meade in uniform.","caption":"George G. Meade commanded a Pennsylvania Reserves brigade at Glendale and was wounded during the close fighting around Randol’s battery.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 165 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/09/25/civil-war-podcast-episode-165/"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: Battle of Glendale/Frayser’s Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/glendalebull.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Glendale","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va020b"},{"label":"National Park Service Civil War Series: The Battles for Richmond, 1862","url":"https://npshistory.com/publications/civil_war_series/21/sec6.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records of the Union and Confederate Armies, Series I, Volume XI, Part II","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/013"},{"label":"Historic American Landscapes Survey: Battle of Glendale and White Oak Swamp","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/va2113/"},{"label":"Primary map: Robert Knox Sneden’s plan of Glendale, June 30, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00093/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Alfred R. Waud’s Glendale drawing, June 30, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660207/"},{"label":"Primary visual source: Waud’s drawing of Kearny’s division at Glendale","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660376/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: James Longstreet portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666541/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George G. Meade portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022631585/"},{"label":"Primary source: George A. McCall, “The Seven Days’ Contests”","url":"https://archive.org/details/sevendayscontest00mcca"},{"label":"U.S. War Department Official Army Register for 1862: Seneca G. Simmons casualty record","url":"https://armyofgeorgia.com/sitebuildercontent/sitebuilderfiles/army_register_1862-1865_part1.pdf"},{"label":"Andalusia Historic House: Henry J. Biddle family history","url":"https://andalusiapa.org/biddle-family-history-and-timeline/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Alfred Waud’s Glendale field sketch",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep165-waud-glendale.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Alfred Waud’s Glendale field sketch at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep165-waud-glendale.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of soldiers and the battlefield landscape at Glendale, Virginia, on June 30, 1862.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Alfred Waud’s Glendale field sketch"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alfred R. Waud made this pencil-and-Chinese-white drawing at Glendale on June 30, 1862. The Library of Congress title is supplied from an inscription on the reverse. It is a participant-era visual record, not a surveyed battlefield plan."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep165-waud-glendale.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Was at Haxall’s Landing and aboard USS Galena during the main fighting; reached Fitz John Porter’s Malvern Hill headquarters at about 8:30 p.m."}]]}],["$","div","II Corps-Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"II Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"John Sedgwick’s division supplied Napoleon J. T. Dana’s and Alfred Sully’s brigades to the decisive sector near Willis Church Road"}]]}],["$","div","III Corps-Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"III Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Samuel P. Heintzelman"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Philip Kearny’s division, including John C. Robinson’s brigade, held north of McCall; Joseph Hooker’s division held south and southeast of McCall"}]]}],["$","div","Pennsylvania Reserves division, V Corps-Brig. Gen. George A. McCall",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Pennsylvania Reserves division, V Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George A. McCall"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Brigades of Truman Seymour, George G. Meade, and Col. Seneca G. Simmons held the central sector; McCall was wounded and captured"}]]}],["$","div","1st Division, VI Corps-Brig. Gen. Henry W. Slocum",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Division, VI Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Henry W. Slocum"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Held Huger on the Charles City Road and sent George W. Taylor’s New Jersey brigade to strike Field’s flank"}]]}],["$","div","Battery B, 1st Pennsylvania Light Artillery, and Batteries E and G, 1st U.S. Artillery-Capts. James H. Cooper and Alanson M. Randol",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Battery B, 1st Pennsylvania Light Artillery, and Batteries E and G, 1st U.S. Artillery"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Capts. James H. Cooper and Alanson M. Randol"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Their gun lines were the focus of repeated attacks by Jenkins, Wilcox, and Field"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Confederacy",{"className":"command-column #9a3d32","children":["$L1f",["$L20","$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]]}]]}],"$L28"]]]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Col. Seneca G. Simmons",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Col. Seneca G. Simmons"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander, 1st Brigade, Pennsylvania Reserves division","."]}]," ","Fatally wounded or killed while leading the counterattack against Kemper’s brigade. Contemporary and later records differ on whether he died on the field or during the following night; the official army register records his death at the June 30 battle."]}]]}],["$","article","Capt. Henry J. Biddle",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Capt. Henry J. Biddle"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Assistant adjutant general, Pennsylvania Reserves division","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded at Glendale and captured. He was taken to Chimborazo Hospital in Richmond and died on July 20, 1862."]}]]}]]}]]}]
